<TeXmacs|2.1.5>

<style|<tuple|article|doc>>

<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|Using plug-ins to insert technical
  figures>|<doc-running-title|Using plug-ins to insert technical
  figures>|<doc-author|<author-data|<author-name|Nicolas
  Ratier>>>|<doc-misc|Updated for <TeXmacs> 2.1.5 in 2026.>>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><TeXmacs> plug-ins

    <item><name|DraTeX>/<name|AlDraTeX>

    <item><name|Gnuplot>

    <item><name|Graphviz>

    <item><name|Xy-pic>
  </enumerate>

  <section|<TeXmacs> plug-ins>

  <TeXmacs> communicates with external programs through <em|plug-ins>. Each
  plug-in is a folder in the <verbatim|plugins> directory of <TeXmacs> (on
  <name|macOS>, inside the application: <verbatim|TeXmacs.app/Contents/Resources/share/TeXmacs/plugins>);
  you can add your own plug-ins in the folder <verbatim|plugins> of your
  <TeXmacs> home folder (<verbatim|~/.TeXmacs> on <name|Linux> and
  <name|macOS>, <verbatim|%APPDATA%\\TeXmacs> on <name|Windows>). A plug-in
  usually needs an external program (a computer algebra system, <LaTeX>,
  <name|Gnuplot>, ...): <TeXmacs> looks for it in the <verbatim|PATH>, and
  only the plug-ins whose program was found appear in the menu
  <menu|Insert|Session>. The result of this detection is kept in a cache, and
  is redone only when the folders of the <verbatim|PATH> change. This menu
  starts a <em|session>, in which you type commands for the program and get
  its answers, formulas or pictures, right in your document.

  <\big-figure|<image|images/insert-session-menu.png|0.25par|||>>
    The menu <menu|Insert|Session>, with the plug-ins found on this computer.
  </big-figure>

  If you install a program after <TeXmacs>, or if it is not in the
  <verbatim|PATH>, use <menu|Insert|Session|Redetect> to empty this cache and
  look for the programs again, and, on <name|macOS> and <name|Windows>,
  <menu|Insert|Session|Manual path> to tell <TeXmacs> where a program is.
  Some plug-ins have options in <menu|Insert|Session|Preferences>.

  Among the plug-ins of <TeXmacs> 2.1.5, we find:

  <\itemize>
    <item>Computer algebra systems

    <\center>
      <block|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|axiom, fricas>|<cell|The <name|Axiom>
      system and its successor <name|FriCAS>>>|<row|<cell|cadabra>|<cell|Field
      theory>>|<row|<cell|cocoa5>|<cell|Commutative
      algebra>>|<row|<cell|giac>|<cell|The computer algebra system of
      <name|Xcas>>>|<row|<cell|gtybalt>|<cell|Built on
      <name|GiNaC>>>|<row|<cell|macaulay2>|<cell|Algebraic geometry and
      commutative algebra>>|<row|<cell|maple>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|mathemagix>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|mathematica>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|maxima>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|mupad>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|mycas>|<cell|A
      tiny example plug-in for developers>>|<row|<cell|pari>|<cell|Number
      theory>>|<row|<cell|reduce>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|sage>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|sympy>|<cell|Symbolic
      mathematics in <name|Python>>>|<row|<cell|yacas>|<cell|>>>>>
    </center>

    <item>Numerical computation software

    <\center>
      <block|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|lush>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|matlab>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|octave>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|python>|<cell|>>|<row|<cell|qcl>|<cell|Simulation
      of quantum algorithms>>|<row|<cell|r>|<cell|Statistical
      computing>>|<row|<cell|scilab>|<cell|>>>>>
    </center>

    <item><with|color|red|Drawing programs><no-page-break*>

    <\center>
      <with|color|red|<block|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|asymptote>|<cell|Vector
      graphics language>>|<row|<cell|dratex>|<cell|Drawing different types of
      charts and diagrams>>|<row|<cell|eukleides>|<cell|Euclidean
      geometry>>|<row|<cell|feynmf>|<cell|Feynman
      diagrams>>|<row|<cell|gnuplot>|<cell|Data and function
      plotting>>|<row|<cell|graph>|<cell|One session for several of these
      programs>>|<row|<cell|graphviz>|<cell|Automatic graph
      visualization>>|<row|<cell|plantuml>|<cell|UML
      diagrams>>|<row|<cell|texgraph>|<cell|Mathematical
      graphics>>|<row|<cell|tikz>|<cell|<name|PGF>/<name|TikZ>
      pictures>>|<row|<cell|xypic>|<cell|Commutative diagrams>>>>>>
    </center>

    <item>Other plug-ins: shells (shell, powershell), programming languages
    (lisp, the proof assistant coq, jupyter kernels), and the <name|AI>
    plug-in (sessions with language models).
  </itemize>

  This tutorial is about four of the drawing programs (in red). Each of them
  turns a short text description into a picture, which the session inserts in
  the document as a <name|PostScript> image: the picture is saved with the
  document and printed in vector quality. An <name|EPS> picture comes at
  0.618 of the width of the paragraph (0.8 for <name|Gnuplot>); see the
  section on <name|Gnuplot> for changing it. In this document, the small
  pictures of <name|DraTeX> and <name|Xy-pic> were set back to their natural
  size.

  <\big-figure|<image|images/session-window.png|0.75par|||>>
    A <name|DraTeX> session in the window of <TeXmacs>. When the cursor is in
    the session, the focus toolbar shows the commands of the session: move
    between the fields, insert or remove fields, fold or unfold the field,
    the preferences of the plug-in, the help of the plug-in, evaluate,
    interrupt, close the session (the same commands are in the menu
    <menu|Focus>).
  </big-figure>

  <section|<name|DraTeX>/<name|AlDraTeX>>

  <\itemize>
    <item><name|DraTeX> provides commands for drawing basic shapes like
    lines, rectangles, and B\<#E9\>zier curves, and for defining utilities
    that produce more complex outcomes.

    <item><name|AlDraTeX> provides templates for drawing different types of
    charts and diagrams.
  </itemize>

  Start the session with <menu|Insert|Session|DraTex>. It needs <LaTeX> and
  <verbatim|dvips> (and <name|Python>, which runs the plug-ins of the drawing
  programs); the two style files <verbatim|DraTex.sty> and
  <verbatim|AlDraTex.sty> come with the plug-in. Type the drawing between
  <verbatim|\\Draw> and <verbatim|\\EndDraw>; <key|S-return> starts a new
  line in the input, and <key|return> sends the input to <LaTeX>.

  <\session|dratex|default>
    <\output>
      TeXmacs interface to (Al)DraTex (High Level Drawing Facilities)
    </output>

    <\unfolded-io>
      dratex]\ 
    <|unfolded-io>
      \\Draw

      \\Tree()(

      2,atom \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ //

      0,electrons & 2,nucleus \ //

      0,protons \ \ & 0,neutrons //)

      \\EndDraw
    <|unfolded-io>
      <image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>
    </unfolded-io>

    <\unfolded-io>
      dratex]\ 
    <|unfolded-io>
      \\Draw

      \\TreeAlign(H,0,0)(0,0,0)

      \\TreeSpace(S,5,15)

      \\Tree()(

      2,European //

      3,Latin \ & 2,Celtic //

      0,French & 0,Italian & 0,Spanish & 0,Irish & 0,Welsh //)

      \\EndDraw
    <|unfolded-io>
      <image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>
    </unfolded-io>

    <\input>
      dratex]\ 
    <|input>
      \;
    </input>
  </session>

  <section|<name|Gnuplot>>

  <\itemize>
    <item><name|Gnuplot> is a portable command-line driven interactive data
    and function plotting utility. <name|Gnuplot> supports many types of
    plots in either 2D or 3D. It can draw using lines, points, boxes,
    contours, vector fields, surfaces, and various associated text. It also
    supports various specialized plot types.
  </itemize>

  Start the session with <menu|Insert|Session|Gnuplot>; the program
  <verbatim|gnuplot> (and <name|Python>) must be installed, otherwise
  <name|Gnuplot> is not in the menu. Type the commands of <name|Gnuplot>; the
  session sends them to <verbatim|gnuplot> with the terminal
  <verbatim|postscript eps> and inserts the plot.

  <\session|gnuplot|default>
    <\unfolded-io>
      gnuplot]\ 
    <|unfolded-io>
      plot [-3.14:3.14] sin(x)
    <|unfolded-io>
      <image|<tuple|<#>|ps>|0.8par|||>
    </unfolded-io>

    <\input|Gnuplot] >
      \;
    </input>
  </session>

  The plot is inserted at 0.8 of the width of the paragraph. A first line
  starting with <verbatim|%> changes the size or the format of the picture of
  one input: for instance

  <\verbatim-code>
    % -width 0.5par -output svg

    plot [-3.14:3.14] sin(x)
  </verbatim-code>

  makes a smaller plot, in the <name|SVG> format (<verbatim|eps>, the
  default, <verbatim|png> or <verbatim|svg>; the option <verbatim|-height>
  also exists). The width is given in <verbatim|par>, <verbatim|pt> or
  <verbatim|px>, the height in <verbatim|pag>, <verbatim|pt> or
  <verbatim|px>; <name|PNG> and <name|SVG> pictures without a size come at
  their natural size. The options <verbatim|-width> and <verbatim|-height>
  also work in the sessions of <name|DraTeX> and <name|Xy-pic>, which always
  produce <name|EPS>; in the sessions of <name|Graphviz>, the line starts
  with the name of the layout program, as in <verbatim|%dot -width 0.5par
  -output svg>.

  <section|<name|Graphviz>>

  <\itemize>
    <item>Graph visualization is a way of representing structural information
    as diagrams of abstract graphs and networks.

    <item><name|Graphviz> is automatic graph visualization software. The
    <name|Graphviz> layout programs take descriptions of graphs in a simple
    text language, and make diagrams in several useful formats such as
    <name|PostScript> for inclusion in <TeXmacs>.
  </itemize>

  Start the session with <menu|Insert|Session|Graphviz>; the program
  <verbatim|dot> of <name|Graphviz> must be installed. The session starts
  with the layout program <verbatim|dot>; type <verbatim|neato>,
  <verbatim|twopi>, <verbatim|circo>, <verbatim|fdp>, <verbatim|sfdp>,
  <verbatim|patchwork> or <verbatim|osage> alone in an input to switch to
  another layout program (and <verbatim|help> for the list), or start one
  input with a line like <verbatim|%neato> to use it only once.

  <\session|dot|default>
    <\output>
      dot - graphviz version 16.1.0 (20260904.0139)
    </output>

    <\unfolded-io>
      dot]\ 
    <|unfolded-io>
      digraph G {

      \ \ main -\> parse -\> execute;

      \ \ main -\> init;

      \ \ main -\> cleanup;

      \ \ execute -\> make_string;

      \ \ execute -\> printf;

      \ \ init -\> make_string;

      \ \ main -\> printf;

      \ \ execute -\> compare;

      }
    <|unfolded-io>
      <image|<tuple|<#>|ps>|0.618par|||>
    </unfolded-io>

    <\unfolded-io>
      dot]\ 
    <|unfolded-io>
      digraph g {

      \ \ node [shape = record, height = 0.1];

      \ \ node0[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> G \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node1[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> E \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node2[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> B \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node3[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> F \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node4[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> R \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node5[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> H \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node6[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> Y \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node7[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> A \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ node8[label = "\<f0\> \| \<f1\> C \| \<f2\> "];

      \ \ "node0":f2 -\> "node4":f1;

      \ \ "node0":f0 -\> "node1":f1;

      \ \ "node1":f0 -\> "node2":f1;

      \ \ "node1":f2 -\> "node3":f1;

      \ \ "node2":f2 -\> "node8":f1;

      \ \ "node2":f0 -\> "node7":f1;

      \ \ "node4":f2 -\> "node6":f1;

      \ \ "node4":f0 -\> "node5":f1;

      }
    <|unfolded-io>
      <image|<tuple|<#>|ps>|0.618par|||>
    </unfolded-io>

    <\input>
      dot]\ 
    <|input>
      \;
    </input>
  </session>

  In the inputs of the session, the highlighting of the <name|dot> language
  shows each space inside a string as a small open-box mark: type them as
  ordinary spaces.

  <section|<name|Xy-pic>>

  <\itemize>
    <item><name|Xy-pic> is a package for typesetting \Pmatrix-like\Q
    diagrams, such as commutative diagrams. In category theory, \Pcommutative
    diagrams\Q are the categorists' ways to illustrate equations and
    universal properties.
  </itemize>

  Start the session with <menu|Insert|Session|XYpic>; it needs <LaTeX> with
  the package <verbatim|xy> (it is in the usual <TeX> distributions, such as
  <TeX>Live). The input is put in a <LaTeX> document which loads
  <verbatim|xy>, <verbatim|amsmath> and <verbatim|amssymb>.

  <\session|xypic|default>
    <\output>
      TeXmacs interface to XYpic (high level 2-dimensional graphics)
    </output>

    <\unfolded-io>
      xypic]\ 
    <|unfolded-io>
      \\xymatrix{

      \ U \\ar@/_/[ddr]_y \\ar@/^/[drr]^x \\ar@{.\>}[dr]\|-{(x,y)} \\\\

      \ \ & X \\times_Z Y \\ar[d]^q \\ar[r]_p & X \\ar[d]_f \\\\

      \ \ & Y \\ar[r]^g & Z

      }
    <|unfolded-io>
      <image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>
    </unfolded-io>

    <\unfolded-io>
      xypic]\ 
    <|unfolded-io>
      \\xymatrix@!=2.5pc{

      \ X_{d_1(\\theta)} \\ar[rr]^-{\\gamma_\\theta}\\ar@{^(-\>}[d] &&

      \ X_{d_0(\\theta)}\\ar@{^(-\>}[d] \\\\

      \ \\coprod_{\\theta\\in {\\mathcal C}_1}X_{d_1\\theta}\\approx

      \ {\\mathcal C}_1\\times_{{\\mathcal C}_0}X

      \ \\ar[rr]^-\\gamma \\ar[d]_{pr_1=d^*_1x} && X\\ar[d]^x \\\\

      \ {\\mathcal C}_1 \\ar[rr]^{d_0} && {\\mathcal C}_0

      }
    <|unfolded-io>
      <image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>
    </unfolded-io>

    <\input>
      xypic]\ 
    <|input>
      \;
    </input>
  </session>

  <\with|par-mode|right>
    \<copyright\> Nicolas Ratier
  </with>
</body>

